{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Seven days.</b> The sense of \"unleavened bread you shall eat\" is a remembrance of their eating at their going out from Egypt, for He did not command them to eat unleavened bread except that which is eaten with the Paschal offering before midnight, but seven days He commanded to eat unleavened bread as a remembrance of what happened to you at your going out from Egypt, for there it is written \"for it was not leavened,\" and had the Egyptians let them delay even a little they would have leavened their dough. And the seven days, at their going out they ate unleavened bread until Pharaoh sank on the seventh day, for the cloud led them by day and night, and they did not tarry in their encampments a great tarrying. And so it is written concerning the Paschal offering: \"Seven days you shall eat with it unleavened bread, bread of affliction\" (Deuteronomy 16:3). Now behold, concerning the Paschal offering He mentioned, seven days you shall eat unleavened bread — an obligation according to the plain sense, and the witness: \"in order that you remember the day of your going out\" (ibid.) — and on account of this it is written: \"all leavened you shall not eat; in all your dwellings you shall eat unleavened bread\" (Exodus 20) — an obligation in the land of Israel and outside the land." "And since there was a received tradition in the hands of Israel that they would begin to slaughter the Paschal offering when the sun inclines toward the west, and it is written \"you shall not slaughter on leavened the blood of My sacrifice\" (Exodus 34:25), therefore the Sages interpreted it as a division. And know that a completed act — it is not an act. Now behold, the meaning of \"And Elohim completed on the seventh day\" (Genesis 2:2) is: as the seventh day entered, His work was already completed. And likewise \"on the first day you shall remove leaven from your houses\": as the first day enters, all the leaven is already removed from the houses. Now the beginning of the first day is the end of the fourteenth day in the evening, as written \"from the first day until the seventh day.\" And behold, this is its meaning: seven full days they are, from the fourteenth of the month in the evening — which is the end of the fourteenth day — until the twenty-first of the month in the evening. And I will speak further on this."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The verse cited as פ' כ (parashat 20, i.e., Exodus 20) does not in fact contain the quoted phrase in our text of Exodus 20; the reference is uncertain but translated as given."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:33:44.746357261Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}